1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:17,952
The Week Five outline explicitly includes a LUCAS practical. This
lesson uses all seven saved sections of the matching AET supplement,
including its glossary and readiness material. The supplement entry
itself has topic-matched, not explicit-directory, placement.

2
00:00:17,952 --> 00:00:30,960
The aim is to select and integrate mechanical support without losing
useful CPR, correct positioning or patient assessment. Simply getting
the machine onto a manikin is not the whole school objective.

3
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:45,192
Official LUCAS Three version three point one instructions were checked
for the relevant procedures and figures. Actual school version, setup
options and hands-on checkoff still require the instructor and
training unit.

4
00:00:45,192 --> 00:00:59,568
School wording, manufacturer instructions and service permission are
kept distinct. A device lesson does not automatically authorize a PCP
to deploy a device that the current service procedure headings
allocate to advanced crews.

5
00:00:59,568 --> 00:01:11,976
Manual compression quality can deteriorate with fatigue, awkward
positioning or movement. A suitable device can reduce some of those
logistical problems and free attention for other essential tasks.

6
00:01:11,976 --> 00:01:25,536
However, a machine can be misplaced, can fail and can consume valuable
treatment time during application. It is not accurate to describe its
compressions as flawless or its circulation as guaranteed.

7
00:01:25,536 --> 00:01:40,368
Current AHA adult guidance does not recommend routine mechanical CPR
for every arrest. It allows consideration in selected settings where
effective manual compressions are difficult or dangerous, with strict
interruption control.

8
00:01:40,368 --> 00:01:53,232
Therefore explain the specific problem this deployment solves. Device
availability alone is not the indication, and mechanical support is
not a reason to abandon manual CPR competence or renewal.

9
00:01:53,232 --> 00:02:05,760
Coronary perfusion pressure describes the driving pressure for
myocardial blood flow. During CPR it is commonly considered as the
difference between aortic and right atrial pressure during relaxation.

10
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:17,640
That explains why recoil, adequate depth and short interruptions
matter. It does not mean that a routine noninvasive cuff or ETCO2
display directly measures this gradient.

11
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:30,936
The school uses numerical examples for pressure and rebuilding after a
pause. Treat them as physiology illustrations, not a universally
measured minimum that proves this individual will achieve ROSC.

12
00:02:30,936 --> 00:02:44,808
The practical consequence is to minimize avoidable no-flow and observe
actual performance. A piston moving at a regular rate cannot by itself
establish that the myocardium or brain is receiving sufficient blood.

13
00:02:44,808 --> 00:02:59,976
The saved school material teaches broad device knowledge. Current
public PR31 has an ACP-and-above applicability heading and selected
nontraumatic medical-arrest situations. Those facts must remain
visible together.

14
00:02:59,976 --> 00:03:12,624
Manufacturer personnel recommendations also require CPR competence and
specific LUCAS training. Reading this slide is neither the complete
instructions nor a practical performance evaluation.

15
00:03:12,624 --> 00:03:24,624
A school instructor can supervise the required laboratory
demonstration while explaining the later service boundary. Do not
relabel a supervised learner exercise as independent field permission.

16
00:03:24,624 --> 00:03:39,768
If an existing device accompanies a patient, confirm the actual
trained personnel, transport plan and local requirements. The
existence of the device does not automatically make any receiving crew
competent or authorized to operate it.

17
00:03:39,768 --> 00:03:53,328
The manufacturer intended population is an adult in circulatory arrest
where external compressions are expected to help. Confirm the clinical
indication and the more specific service selection before applying the
frame.

18
00:03:53,328 --> 00:04:09,984
For a too-small fit, the specified three quick signals and inability
to enter the required operating state mean manual CPR must resume. The
school seventeen-centimeter reference concerns chest fit, not the
patient standing height or sternum length.

19
00:04:09,984 --> 00:04:23,136
For a too-large fit, do not press the frame down onto the chest merely
to engage the locks. Body weight alone is not the manufacturer fit
test; correct safe placement and closure are what matter.

20
00:04:23,136 --> 00:04:40,392
The supplement calls chest trauma a caution, but local PR31 excludes
traumatic arrest. Pregnancy, unusual anatomy, recent surgery or a
difficult position also require the appropriate specific pathway and
safe fit, not improvised universal permission.

21
00:04:40,392 --> 00:04:54,600
Identify the back plate, upper unit, support legs, claw locks and
release rings on the actual training device. Knowing the name is
useful only if you can identify what must connect and what must stay
unobstructed.

22
00:04:54,600 --> 00:05:07,536
The suction cup contains the pressure pad and is a disposable
component in the reviewed instructions. Its outer edge and the
pressure pad are related but not identical anatomical references.

23
00:05:07,536 --> 00:05:20,568
The stabilization system helps maintain alignment between patient and
device. It is not a rigid spinal immobilizer, and the frame does not
become an approved torso-protection cage after ROSC.

24
00:05:20,568 --> 00:05:32,400
The patient wrist straps and stabilization straps are not lifting
slings. Prepare the actual transport support and team technique rather
than attempting to carry a patient by these straps.

25
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:46,248
ADJUST allows the compression interface to be positioned. PAUSE after
adjustment establishes the start state, while PAUSE during
compressions stops the mechanism at that state. ACTIVE starts the
chosen compression mode.

26
00:05:46,248 --> 00:06:01,152
Those are functional distinctions, not a guarantee of one identical
physical movement on every unit. Version three point one allows setup
options that affect automatic adjustment, rates, ventilation alerts
and pauses.

27
00:06:01,152 --> 00:06:15,024
Keep hands and other body parts away from moving compression
components. A displayed mode must be checked together with the actual
mechanism and patient position, not trusted as the sole sign of
effective CPR.

28
00:06:15,024 --> 00:06:29,904
The original diagram separates normal state changes from the manual
fallback. In particular, a planned battery replacement from PAUSE
returns to PAUSE under the preserved-state conditions, not to ADJUST
as one school sentence claims.

29
00:06:29,904 --> 00:06:44,424
Before deployment, confirm an appropriate charged battery, a ready
spare, correct accessories and an undamaged device. The required
battery remains installed even when an approved external power source
is connected.

30
00:06:44,424 --> 00:06:57,720
Verify the cup and straps, unobstructed ventilation openings and the
availability of the back plate. A charged motor without the plate or
required disposable is not a ready resuscitation system.

31
00:06:57,720 --> 00:07:11,568
Power-up includes a self-test and a ready indication. Know the actual
training device response before the event. A failed self-test is not a
prompt to repeatedly experiment while the patient receives no
compressions.

32
00:07:11,568 --> 00:07:24,480
Preparation also means assigned people and manual backup. The safest
device team is the one that can quickly continue effective manual CPR
when the machine is unavailable or unsuitable.

33
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:37,968
Recognize arrest and start manual CPR immediately; do not wait for the
LUCAS case to open. Effective manual care and prompt indicated
defibrillation remain active while the device is prepared.

34
00:07:37,968 --> 00:07:51,096
Plan who supports the head, places the back plate, attaches the frame,
confirms the cup and restarts effective compressions. Make readiness
and commands audible before any required pause.

35
00:07:51,096 --> 00:08:04,752
The school summary asks for no more than ten seconds of interruption,
while an earlier passage tolerates under twenty. We retain the tighter
stated training target and do not teach twenty seconds as a harmless
allowance.

36
00:08:04,752 --> 00:08:16,584
Practice accurate placement and safe checks together with speed. A
seven-second median from trained teams is not a guarantee for a novice
or permission to skip the fit and lock check.

37
00:08:16,584 --> 00:08:29,544
The reviewed instructions describe a small coordinated upper-body lift
or side-to-side roll to place the back plate immediately below the
armpits. Support the head and use the actual trained team technique.

38
00:08:29,544 --> 00:08:40,464
Positioning the plate accurately now makes central cup placement
easier later. A low or off-center plate should not be accepted merely
because the frame can be clicked onto it.

39
00:08:40,464 --> 00:08:50,112
Resume manual compressions as soon as the plate is placed. Do not hold
the patient without CPR while another rescuer searches the case for
the upper unit.

40
00:08:50,112 --> 00:09:04,536
For unusual anatomy, injury or movement risk, the instructor must
demonstrate the appropriate method on the training setup. This
explanation is not a universal lifting authorization for any patient
or environment.

41
00:09:04,536 --> 00:09:16,536
Open the claw locks using the release rings, then let go so the locks
can engage. Clothing or sheets must not obstruct the connection. Keep
fingers away from the locking mechanism.

42
00:09:16,536 --> 00:09:30,360
The manufacturer describes coordinated near-side attachment while
manual compressions continue, followed by the brief interruption
needed to attach the other side. Learn this with the actual frame and
partner positions.

43
00:09:30,360 --> 00:09:41,976
Listen for engagement and check the joined parts as instructed, rather
than accepting one click as proof that both sides are secure. Never
force closure by precompressing the chest.

44
00:09:41,976 --> 00:09:54,384
If the frame cannot be safely attached, return immediately to manual
CPR. Troubleshooting a latch must not create an extended pause in the
only treatment currently moving blood.

45
00:09:54,384 --> 00:10:06,960
The pressure pad must act at the appropriate sternal compression
point. The reviewed manufacturer figure uses the lower outer edge of
the cup immediately above the end of the sternum as a positioning
reference.

46
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:19,584
Do not confuse that outer rim with the center of the pressure pad or
use the nipple as a universal landmark. Patient anatomy and the actual
device must be identified, not guessed from clothing.

47
00:10:19,584 --> 00:10:29,784
Incorrect position can worsen injury and impair circulation. A rapid
transition with the compression point too low is not a successful
practical performance.

48
00:10:29,784 --> 00:10:42,648
Show the instructor both the landmark and your reason for it, then
verify after the start and after every meaningful movement. Correct
initial placement is not proof that the device will remain correct.

49
00:10:42,648 --> 00:10:54,792
In the reviewed default workflow, adjust the cup until the pressure
pad contacts the chest without pressing it down. Then use PAUSE to
establish the start position before activating the selected mode.

50
00:10:54,792 --> 00:11:06,192
Version three point one has optional automatic fitting and height
behavior. QuickFit is not a promise that an incorrectly centered
device or an unsafe patient fit will correct itself.

51
00:11:06,192 --> 00:11:19,080
Check the actual state and location before starting, keep hands clear
and observe the initial compressions. If the cup shifts or the start
position is wrong, use the correct adjustment sequence promptly.

52
00:11:19,080 --> 00:11:31,680
If safe correct positioning cannot be achieved, restore manual CPR
instead of repeatedly pressing ACTIVE. A device refusing a state can
be a safety signal, not a control to defeat.

53
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:46,056
Apply the stabilization strap once the device is operating correctly,
avoiding a new unnecessary compression pause. The reviewed arrangement
places a cushion behind the neck near the shoulders and connects it to
the support straps.

54
00:11:46,056 --> 00:11:56,736
This is not an instruction to wrap or tighten a restraint around the
airway. Demonstrate the actual cushion, buckle path and appropriate
securing on the training device.

55
00:11:56,736 --> 00:12:08,712
Prevent twisting, hold the device appropriately during securing and
verify that the cup remains at the intended point. A strap can
stabilize a wrong position just as effectively as a right one.

56
00:12:08,712 --> 00:12:21,840
The manufacturer explicitly allows delaying strap application if it
prevents or delays needed medical treatment. Keep the priority on
effective resuscitation rather than completing every accessory first.

57
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:35,616
The default thirty-to-two mode pauses after thirty compressions to
allow two breaths. The airway operator must anticipate the pause and
deliver appropriate breaths rather than beginning preparation only
when the machine stops.

58
00:12:35,616 --> 00:12:48,648
Continuous mode supports ongoing compressions with asynchronous
ventilation under the appropriate advanced-airway pathway. The
reviewed default alert is ten times per minute, but actual settings
can differ.

59
00:12:48,648 --> 00:13:04,152
A tube or SGA label does not prove effective ventilation. Reassess
seal, chest rise, airway position and waveform; a significant leak or
failed breath requires a coordinated change, not simply faster
bagging.

60
00:13:04,152 --> 00:13:16,104
If the airway plan changes, communicate and select the appropriate
compression mode with the team. The timing light does not assess chest
rise and cannot protect the patient from excessive volume.

61
00:13:16,104 --> 00:13:27,840
Mechanical compressions create ECG artifact. The reviewed LUCAS
instructions require a brief PAUSE before ECG analysis, with the
interruption kept as short as possible.

62
00:13:27,840 --> 00:13:43,368
The manufacturer permits defibrillation during device operation when
the defibrillator instructions and local protocol allow it. That does
not mean the rhythm can be reliably analyzed during compressions, or
that people may keep touching the patient during discharge.

63
00:13:43,368 --> 00:13:57,576
The school drill pauses, analyzes, clears, shocks if indicated and
immediately restarts. Preserve the locally instructed sequence and
distinguish its steps from the broader manufacturer capability
statement.

64
00:13:57,576 --> 00:14:10,848
Keep pads and wires outside the compression contact area, check
position after a shock and explicitly confirm that compressions have
resumed. Do not leave the machine paused while everybody watches the
ECG.

65
00:14:10,848 --> 00:14:26,328
Mechanical support can operate during selected movement if patient and
device are safely supported and the correct position and angle remain
maintained. This is a condition, not a blanket instruction to carry
every arrest immediately.

66
00:14:26,328 --> 00:14:41,472
The school passage suggests a pause for critical transfers; the
manufacturer allows ongoing compressions when its conditions are met.
Plan the actual move and any necessary interruption rather than adding
a routine pause without a reason.

67
00:14:41,472 --> 00:14:55,968
Use the trained lifting and transport method, not wrist straps or
stabilization straps as handles. Protect airway tubing, vascular
access, the head and the team, and observe for shifting or blocked
ventilation openings.

68
00:14:55,968 --> 00:15:07,944
If safe alignment cannot be maintained, act immediately to restore
effective appropriate CPR. Patient movement is not evidence that the
device maintained good compressions throughout the move.

69
00:15:07,944 --> 00:15:19,896
The reviewed manufacturer gives a typical initial battery runtime of
about forty-five minutes for a nominal patient. Do not assume every
battery in service will provide exactly that duration.

70
00:15:19,896 --> 00:15:31,728
Watch the actual battery and alarm indications. Distinguish an
intermittent low-charge warning from the end-of-service-life
indication and from a high-priority stopped-device alarm.

71
00:15:31,728 --> 00:15:43,776
The device requires its approved battery even with external power. Use
only the specified compatible source and protect cables; an improvised
power connection is not a safe backup.

72
00:15:43,776 --> 00:16:00,360
The school gives a single two-hour recharge estimate, but the reviewed
IFU distinguishes charging in the device from the external charger,
with different maximum times. Read the actual charging instructions
and verify charge instead of relying on elapsed time.

73
00:16:00,360 --> 00:16:12,696
Prepare the charged replacement before the swap. In the reviewed
planned sequence, press PAUSE, remove the old battery, insert the
charged battery and wait for the green PAUSE indication.

74
00:16:12,696 --> 00:16:26,808
The school sentence claiming the unit returns to ADJUST at its
previous height is incorrect for this preserved-state sequence. The
manufacturer table returns PAUSE to PAUSE when the battery is replaced
within the specified memory interval.

75
00:16:26,808 --> 00:16:43,800
That memory interval is less than sixty seconds; it is not an
acceptable sixty-second CPR pause. If it is exceeded, a self-test and
resetting the start position are required. Maintain a manual fallback
rather than watching the clock with no compressions.

76
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:58,224
Verify the actual state and cup position, then restart the appropriate
ACTIVE mode promptly. A routine planned swap differs from stopping a
malfunctioning device, where removal and immediate manual CPR take
priority.

77
00:16:58,224 --> 00:17:11,424
A high-priority malfunction can stop compressions. The reviewed
warning directs stopping the device, removing it and starting manual
compressions immediately if operation is abnormal or compressions are
insufficient.

78
00:17:11,424 --> 00:17:25,080
Do not allow a prolonged no-flow interval while several rescuers
troubleshoot. Assign a focused equipment task while effective manual
care continues, and use qualified service support for a persistent
fault.

79
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:38,184
A mute control silences a signal for a period; it does not fix the
fault, recharge a battery or restart circulation. Distinguish an
audible warning from proof of actual compression performance.

80
00:17:38,184 --> 00:17:53,760
Repositioning a shifted cup and responding to a failed motor are
different problems. If safe, prompt correction cannot restore a
reliable state, use manual CPR rather than repeatedly trying to make
the machine appear normal.

81
00:17:53,760 --> 00:18:08,664
When spontaneous circulation is appropriately confirmed, stop
unnecessary mechanical compressions and announce the post-arrest
transition. An organized rhythm or a compression-generated pulse alone
is not that confirmation.

82
00:18:08,664 --> 00:18:21,984
The school calls leaving the locked device on every post-ROSC patient
a gold standard. The reviewed IFU does not establish that universal
claim, so it is not taught as a mandatory rule here.

83
00:18:21,984 --> 00:18:37,392
If the device remains temporarily for the authorized plan, ensure it
does not impair ventilation, chest expansion or comfort, and keep
checking the patient and actual state. Follow the relevant team
decision for adjustment or removal.

84
00:18:37,392 --> 00:18:49,872
A prior height setting must be reverified after patient or device
movement before any later restart. Being one button away does not
remove the need to confirm re-arrest, fit and position.

85
00:18:49,872 --> 00:19:03,456
Effective CPR can sometimes support enough cerebral blood flow for
movement or apparent awareness without sustained spontaneous
circulation. The school glossary names this CPR-induced consciousness.

86
00:19:03,456 --> 00:19:15,144
Treat a new movement as an important patient change requiring
appropriate assessment, not automatic proof that the arrest has ended.
Distinguish it from a mechanical twitch during shock delivery.

87
00:19:15,144 --> 00:19:30,864
Current N02 places consideration of sedation for CPR-induced
consciousness in the ACP interventions. It is not an independent PCP
drug instruction, and paralysis alone would not provide comfort or
unconsciousness.

88
00:19:30,864 --> 00:19:42,936
Coordinate effective care, protect the patient and team and obtain the
appropriate support. Do not simply switch off all circulation support
or silence distress to make the device workflow easier.

89
00:19:42,936 --> 00:19:55,656
Removal may follow a planned transition, unsafe fit or malfunction.
State which situation applies because the urgent manual fallback and a
controlled post-event removal have different priorities.

90
00:19:55,656 --> 00:20:09,792
The reviewed removal sequence powers off, disconnects the
stabilization neck section and releases the upper part from the plate
using the release rings. Use the actual unit and avoid trapping lines
or body parts.

91
00:20:09,792 --> 00:20:20,232
Remove the back plate only if the patient condition allows it. Do not
disrupt airway, perfusion support or a safe transport plan simply to
complete equipment cleanup.

92
00:20:20,232 --> 00:20:32,208
When CPR is still indicated and the machine cannot safely provide it,
manual compressions resume immediately. Equipment handling must be
coordinated around that clinical priority.

93
00:20:32,208 --> 00:20:45,984
The reviewed LUCAS Three instructions and school supplement direct
replacing the used disposable suction cup. Older PR31 wording
discusses cleaning and reuse, so that conflict must not be hidden.

94
00:20:45,984 --> 00:21:02,568
Use the actual approved device and infection-control instructions. For
reusable surfaces and straps, use the compatible process and
disinfectant handling directions; do not infer a mixing recipe or
contact time from an ambiguous bleach label.

95
00:21:02,568 --> 00:21:14,616
Do not immerse the device or allow liquid into the housing. Inspect
for damage, let appropriate components dry, replace required
disposables and recharge or replace the battery.

96
00:21:14,616 --> 00:21:28,464
A cleaned appearance is not the entire readiness check. Verify
completeness, correct cup attachment, straps, open locks, power and
the self-test according to the routine and post-use checks.

97
00:21:28,464 --> 00:21:42,120
Document when manual and mechanical compressions began or stopped, why
the device was selected, the operating modes and significant
interruptions. Do not count a silent stopped machine as ongoing CPR.

98
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:56,256
Include fit or position problems, movement checks, battery changes,
alarms, fallback and the patient response. A device record helps but
cannot establish every clinical finding or human action automatically.

99
00:21:56,256 --> 00:22:11,616
The reviewed device can send status/use data when powered off,
depending on configuration. Transmission can also receive settings, so
use the authorized process; this course does not upload patient data
or change a real device.

100
00:22:11,616 --> 00:22:26,160
For a fault, follow reporting, quarantine and qualified service
procedures rather than returning an unreliable unit to the case.
Record readiness honestly so the next crew does not inherit a hidden
problem.

101
00:22:26,160 --> 00:22:42,096
In this original supervised station, start effective manual CPR on a
suitable adult manikin. Prepare and apply the device using named
roles, anatomical checks and measured interruptions, not just a total
assembly time.

102
00:22:42,096 --> 00:22:54,456
Next introduce an unsafe fit. A good performance is recognizing the
limit and promptly maintaining manual CPR, not forcing a successful-
looking installation for the assessor.

103
00:22:54,456 --> 00:23:06,720
Then rehearse a planned battery replacement and state what indicator
must return before restart. Finally introduce a stopped-device alarm
and time how quickly effective manual support returns.

104
00:23:06,720 --> 00:23:19,776
Review position, ventilation, analysis, safety and communication
alongside timing. Improve one specific weakness and repeat; a short
but unsafe run is not a pass.

105
00:23:19,776 --> 00:24:17,040
The recovered April 2026 compilation has a second, literal page track.
Page 13 ends PR05 decontamination; 85 ends PR31 and 86 is PR32
vascular compression band; 581-584 continue N02, 585-587 contain N03,
588 opens N04 traumatic arrest, and 614-615 continue R02 with its
diagram. These thirteen pages were read separately from the five named
assignments. PR06 is still taught as a named procedure even though
that numbered span does not contain its full standalone page. An index
collision is not evidence that decontamination or a radial band is
part of the N02 drug algorithm.

106
00:24:17,040 --> 00:25:06,360
The decontamination ending explains a chain: move away from the
hazard, safely remove contaminated clothing, reduce contamination and
actively dry after wet methods. Emergency removal is immediate; gross
decontamination uses larger water systems, and technical
decontamination needs specialist resources. The source describes dry
absorption as the default and selected wet methods with water no
hotter than forty Celsius. That is not a command to apply water to
every unknown chemical. Use incident-specific advice, protected
specialists and safe access; communicate clearly and preserve warmth,
dignity and assistance for people who cannot self-decontaminate. Do
not bring secondary contamination into the ambulance.

107
00:25:06,360 --> 00:25:50,616
A vascular compression band controls a radial access site after trans-
radial angiography; it is not a limb tourniquet for major traumatic
bleeding. Learn two competing concerns: puncture-site hemostasis and
adequate hand perfusion. Establish baseline observations and inspect
pain, tingling, numbness, temperature, colour, refill, thumb oximetry
and bleeding or hematoma. The recovered procedure specifies fifteen-
minute checks. A thumb saturation number alone cannot exclude a
vascular problem. During transfer, preserve the ordered wrist
restrictions, comfort, access and monitoring and communicate new
abnormalities promptly.

108
00:25:50,616 --> 00:26:33,096
For the specific band procedure, verify application time, the release-
start instruction and the documented starting air volume before
attempting release. The source describes a usual fifteen millilitres,
an eighteen-millilitre maximum and removal of two millilitres every
fifteen minutes with site reassessment. These are device/procedure-
specific figures, not an instruction to inflate any wrist band to
fifteen. If bleeding occurs during deflation, stop and restore the
amount just removed under the applicable plan. Use the matching
syringe and current instructions; an unclear product or handover
requires clarification, not guessed deflation.

109
00:26:33,096 --> 00:27:27,456
The August 2026 PR32 card adds explicit complication pathways. For
bleeding it limits inflation to eighteen millilitres; uncontrolled
bleeding at that limit leads to band removal and proximal direct
pressure, with hospital diversion if bleeding persists beyond ten
minutes. It says not to reapply the band. With a hematoma and band
still on, mark its extent, leave the band in place, apply directed
pressure for ten minutes or longer and notify. Neurovascular
compromise has a distinct one-millilitre release approach while
hemostasis is preserved; lack of improvement prompts the nearest
hospital. The card also allows a deflated band to remain during
transfer for possible re-inflation. Document and notify the receiving
team; do not improvise one adjustment for every complication.

110
00:27:27,456 --> 00:28:10,920
The N04 opening shifts the clinical mechanism: hemorrhage, hypoxemia
and obstructed circulation can drive traumatic arrest, and a medical
collapse can also precede the injury. Address the immediately relevant
reversible cause and airway or bleeding problem in parallel, rather
than allowing a mechanical-compression project to displace them. The
source calls for consultation about treatment and conveyance and
refers to trauma destination criteria. An organized rhythm without a
pulse does not prove adequate flow, and a poor-prognosis association
is not a complete discontinuation rule. PR31 excludes mechanical CPR
in traumatic arrest.

111
00:28:10,920 --> 00:28:57,792
Read N04 general-body procedures with the provider-specific sections.
The current PCP section says consider vascular access and a twenty-
millilitre-per-kilogram bolus for hypovolemia; chest needle
decompression appears under ACP, not as independent PCP authority. The
opening also describes a selected penetrating-chest-trauma, PEA and
short-to-trauma-centre situation where airway procedures must not
delay conveyance, with fifteen-minute and Vancouver Coastal-Urban
twenty-minute wording. These qualifications are not a universal clock
for every injury. Escalate early, use the actual trauma pathway and
reassess volume response rather than giving every arrest the same
fluid or invasive treatment.

112
00:28:57,792 --> 00:29:40,416
PR06 describes adult breaths of no more than five hundred to six
hundred millilitres during CPR and a five-centimetre compression
reference. They sit alongside rate, recoil, minimal pauses and the
airway-dependent ventilation pattern. The practical objective is
effective chest rise without overventilation, not emptying the whole
bag to hit a memorized volume. Current adult depth teaching elsewhere
uses five to six centimetres with avoidance of excess depth. PR06
palpation during ongoing compressions assesses generated flow; only
the appropriately timed assessment of spontaneous circulation can
establish ROSC.

113
00:29:40,416 --> 00:30:26,976
Complete the N03 transition beyond the displayed oxygen targets.
Reassess airway protection, glucose, pressure, temperature and the
precipitating cause; its twenty-millilitre-per-kilogram fluid
framework is a limit/context, not a routine volume for every patient.
It describes thirty-degree head elevation where feasible, passive
rather than large-volume cold-fluid cooling for suitable nontraumatic
patients, and no passive cooling after traumatic arrest. Its
destination guidance distinguishes suspected cardiac cause or STEMI
and feasible PCI access from suspected noncardiac cause. Ongoing
instability, the actual destination pathway and consultation still
matter.

